WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.302
- [N.G.] While we would like to think

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:06.045
that we can predict people's behavior,

00:00:06.045 --> 00:00:08.683
we can't always do that.

00:00:08.683 --> 00:00:11.433
People behave in unexpected ways.

00:00:12.398 --> 00:00:15.833
They do things that are
unthinkable and unimaginable.

00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:17.650
And thus the question becomes

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:18.927
"How do you understand that?",

00:00:18.927 --> 00:00:21.427
"How do you dissect behavior?"

00:00:24.278 --> 00:00:25.825
- [Narrator] The Hulu
original series Chance

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:28.493
paints the portrait of a
forensic neuropsychiatrist

00:00:28.493 --> 00:00:31.219
who embarks on his own dark journey.

00:00:31.219 --> 00:00:33.141
We take a closer look at the new thriller

00:00:33.141 --> 00:00:37.141
with the help of a real-life
neuropsychologist.

00:00:39.387 --> 00:00:42.172
(suspenseful music)

00:00:42.172 --> 00:00:44.209
(lamp clicks)

00:00:44.209 --> 00:00:45.774
- My name is Doctor N.G. Berrill.

00:00:45.774 --> 00:00:48.217
I'm a forensic neuropsychologist

00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:50.384
and essentially I focus on

00:00:52.441 --> 00:00:55.753
decoding the puzzle of human behavior.

00:00:55.753 --> 00:00:56.924
Quite often we meet people

00:00:56.924 --> 00:01:00.176
who function according to societal norms

00:01:00.176 --> 00:01:02.958
and slowly over time, they
deteriorate in some way,

00:01:02.958 --> 00:01:05.853
where they make choices which are peculiar

00:01:05.853 --> 00:01:08.225
or self-destructive.

00:01:08.225 --> 00:01:10.626
- [Hugh] Eldon Chance is a 55 year-old,

00:01:10.626 --> 00:01:13.448
right-handed neuropsychiatrist.

00:01:13.448 --> 00:01:15.339
Of late, he is increasingly aware

00:01:15.339 --> 00:01:19.442
of a mental state he finds
to be dark and unstable.

00:01:19.442 --> 00:01:20.539
- And those people are interesting

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:23.305
because their life doesn't follow a path

00:01:23.305 --> 00:01:26.555
that's easily predicted and understood.

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.143
Everyone has a story.

00:01:29.143 --> 00:01:31.764
They got to be where they are

00:01:31.764 --> 00:01:34.400
owing to a host of factors.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:39.174
- I spend my days in the company
of those mutilated by life.

00:01:39.174 --> 00:01:41.833
- As a clinical neuropsychologist,
the areas of the brain

00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:45.549
that are of most importance
would be the limbic system

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:49.250
and prefrontal cortex,
or the prefrontal lobe.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:53.490
The limbic system regulates
human emotion, affective memory,

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:56.690
our capacity to become
angry, even aggressive.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:00.056
So, for example, if we
have a perceived threat,

00:02:00.056 --> 00:02:03.059
that may activate the limbic system.

00:02:03.059 --> 00:02:05.309
(grunting)

00:02:07.239 --> 00:02:11.406
Now it's the prefrontal area
that perceive this activation

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.976
and then help us to determine
how we wanna proceed.

00:02:16.976 --> 00:02:20.183
If the prefrontal lobes
are not working properly,

00:02:20.183 --> 00:02:22.000
you can have a lot of trouble.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.030
There can be really a bad situation.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:24.863
(cars honking)

00:02:24.863 --> 00:02:26.383
- [Eldon] Since his
discharge, the patient admits

00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:30.050
to both visual and
olfactory hallucinations.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:34.849
- When we look at ourselves,
we always have explanations

00:02:34.849 --> 00:02:37.891
and rationalizations for why we
don't behave as we should have.

00:02:37.891 --> 00:02:39.941
People don't always behave rationally,

00:02:39.941 --> 00:02:41.395
and they're not always responsible.

00:02:41.395 --> 00:02:45.240
- Do you really still not see
what dangerous ground this is?

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.571
Or is that what you like about it?

00:02:47.571 --> 00:02:48.826
- Certainly there are
a number of challenges

00:02:48.826 --> 00:02:52.395
in doing forensic neuropsychology
or forensic psychology.

00:02:52.395 --> 00:02:55.770
Sometimes we put ourselves in situations,

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:59.617
which there is some potential
risk or some potential danger.

00:02:59.617 --> 00:03:02.519
Maybe that's what makes it
a little exciting as well,

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:05.436
the unpredictable nature of it all.

00:03:07.922 --> 00:03:10.422
(lamp clicks)

